#: ../../topics/modules.rst:5
msgid "Modules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/modules.rst:7
msgid ""
"ANet is built from a small kernel and a set of optional modules. This "
"page lists the modules, shows which ones a node includes, and describes "
"how to turn a module off, how to build a node without one, and what a "
"node does when a module is absent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/modules.rst:17
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/modules.rst:19
msgid ""
"The *kernel* holds what every node needs: the protocol, identity, "
"encryption, delivery confirmation, storage and admission. The kernel "
"cannot be removed. Everything else is a *module*: a feature that a node "
"can have or lack without losing the ability to exchange tasks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/modules.rst:24
msgid "The design follows three rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/modules.rst:26
msgid ""
"A module reaches the kernel only through a fixed interface, and the "
"kernel grants it only what it needs. A module does not import the daemon,"
" and the kernel does not import a module. Tests in the repository enforce"
" both directions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/modules.rst:30
msgid ""
"A feature that acts for a network peer or spends money is off at run time"
" until the operator turns it on. Including a module in a build does not "
"turn it on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/modules.rst:33
msgid ""
"A module that is absent fails closed. It is not started, it registers "
"nothing, and it accepts nothing from the network. To a peer, the node "
"behaves as if the feature were switched off."
msgstr ""

